 {
  "id": "M31/p4",
  "lesson": "M31",
  "kind": "prediction",
  "prompt": "<p>Random-features regression on 100 training points, with the minimum-norm least-squares fit, for every number of features from 1 to 200. Where is the test error worst?</p>",
  "answer": "a",
  "answerKind": "choice",
  "tol": 0,
  "options": [
   {
    "key": "a",
    "html": "<p>At 100 features, exactly the number of training points.</p>"
   },
   {
    "key": "b",
    "html": "<p>At 200 features, the most complex model.</p>"
   },
   {
    "key": "c",
    "html": "<p>At 1 feature, the simplest.</p>"
   },
   {
    "key": "d",
    "html": "<p>Nowhere in particular: it rises steadily.</p>"
   }
  ],
  "explain": "<p>At 100 features: the interpolation threshold. There the fit must pass through every noisy point with a single solution, whose norm, and variance, explode. With more features the minimum-norm interpolant spreads the fit out and the error falls again. A small ridge penalty removes the peak.</p>"
 },
 {
  "id": "M31/p5",
  "lesson": "M31",
  "kind": "prediction",
  "prompt": "<p>A thousand models that only guess are evaluated on one test set of 1,000 examples, and the best is reported. What accuracy does it report?</p>",
  "answer": "c",
  "answerKind": "choice",
  "tol": 0,
  "options": [
   {
    "key": "a",
    "html": "<p>About 50 percent.</p>"
   },
   {
    "key": "b",
    "html": "<p>About 52 percent.</p>"
   },
   {
    "key": "c",
    "html": "<p>About 55 percent.</p>"
   },
   {
    "key": "d",
    "html": "<p>About 70 percent.</p>"
   }
  ],
  "explain": "<p>About 55 percent: the maximum of 1,000 noisy estimates with standard error <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1.6</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">1.6</span></span></span></span> points sits about <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3.2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3.2</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">3.2</span></span></span></span> standard errors above the truth. A single-model Hoeffding interval, <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>±</mo><mn>4.3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pm 4.3</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">±</span><span class=\"mord\">4.3</span></span></span></span> points, excludes the true 50 percent; the union bound's <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>±</mo><mn>7.3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pm 7.3</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">±</span><span class=\"mord\">7.3</span></span></span></span> does not. Combining models adaptively can push the reported score far higher still.</p>"
 },
 {
  "id": "M31/concept1",
  "lesson": "M31",
  "kind": "concept",
  "prompt": "Why does picking the best of many models on one test set make the winner look better than it is?",
  "answer": "Each model's test accuracy is its true accuracy plus noise of size about 1/sqrt(n). Picking the maximum picks the model whose noise happened to be largest, so the winner's score is biased upward by about sqrt(2 ln k) standard errors for k models. A single-model interval no longer applies; the union bound widens it to sqrt(ln(2k/delta)/(2n)), or a fresh test set gives an unbiased number.",
  "answerKind": "text"
 },
